Durarara!! AU
Chapter 25 – Part 2 
Curtis took another drag, unhurried, blood still marking the line from hairline to jaw.
“I never got to meet a person like you.” The cigarette tip glowed. Long black braids shifted as mismatched eyes held hers without flinching. “Could you perhaps tell me when your obsession started with him?”
The question sat in the leather quiet like a scalpel.
Namie did not look away. For a stretch of street the only movement was the soft burn of tobacco and the city sliding past the windows. When she answered, the corporate ice had thinned into something older — childhood, isolation, a house that had stopped being a home.
“…You do not get a full autobiography because you survived a gun.” Quiet. Precise. “But if you are going to keep using that word, you can have the shape of it.”
A short breath.
“Our parents lost their footing when he was small. Work. Clients. The usual collapse. They were busy surviving their own mess and left the two of us to each other. I was the one who kept him fed, kept him upright, kept the nights from eating him. Isolation does things. It teaches you that the only real person in the room is the one who was always there.” Her eyes stayed on his. “What you call obsession started as the only reliable thing in a house that had stopped being reliable. It did not stay simple. It never does when the world is fake and one person is not.”
A thinner pause.
“Years later I made a mistake. I showed him something in our uncle’s study that I thought would interest a child. It interested him too much. It took a piece of him I have been trying to take back ever since. That is the other half you keep fishing for, Curtis Nightstar — not a brochure about research and parties. A boy who was mine first, and a thing that taught him how to look past me.”
She tipped her chin at the cigarette.
“You wanted a timeline. There it is. Childhood. Neglect. A study. A choice I cannot unmake. You do not get the name of the thing in the jar. Not yet. Not for a smile and a threat to crash my car.”
Smoke found the cracked window.
Namie had given him the origin without surrendering the head, the lab, or the full map of Seiji’s current disappearance — and Curtis Nightstar sat in the front seat with a pack burning down and a sister who had finally admitted the obsession had a birthday.
Curtis exhaled smoke toward the cracked window and did not smile.
“How sad.” The words came out quiet, almost careful. Long black braids shifted as mismatched eyes found hers without the edge he had worn for the gun and the threats. “I’m sorry to hear that, Miss Namie.”
The car went very still.
Namie had been braced for another probe — taboo, crash, inventory, the next cut at the nerve she had just exposed. She had not been braced for apology. For a foreign student with blood on his face and a cigarette between his fingers to look at the childhood she had handed him and call it sad like it cost him something to say it.
Her mouth opened. Closed. The corporate ice tried to reform and found a crack it had not planned for.
“…What.”
Not a question with teeth. A genuine lag. The woman who priced liabilities and threw men at problems had just been offered sympathy by the liability, and the machinery behind her eyes had to stop and recheck the input.
Curtis did not press the advantage. He took another drag, gaze steady, the apology still sitting in the air between them like it belonged there.
“You grew up keeping him alive in a house that stopped being a house,” he said, still quiet. “Then you showed him something that took a piece of him. That’s not a brochure. That’s a wound with a timeline. I’m allowed to be sorry you got stuck with it.”
Namie stared at him.
For the first time since the morning sidewalk, the look she gave Curtis Nightstar was not inventory, not threat, and not the cold math of an open debt. It was the look of someone who had spent years treating every soft sentence as a lie — and had just been hit with one that did not feel like theater.
“…You are a very strange boy,” she said at last, voice lower than before.
Outside, Ikebukuro kept moving.
Inside, the second half of the truth had bought him something neither of them had put on the table: a moment where Namie Yagiri did not know what to do with kindness.
Curtis tapped ash toward the cracked window.
“My apologies for my actions.” The words came without the edge he had used on the gunman. Long black braids shifted as he glanced at the blood still marking his hairline, then back to her. “But when we met this morning, it was nothing but hostility. After learning a bit more… I’m not saying it’s okay. But I understand. You know what I mean, Miss Namie?”
Namie watched him for a long second.
The apology landed in the same strange register as the sympathy — not theater, not a bargain dressed as manners. A foreign student who had put her men on the pavement, survived a gun, thrown a peon into traffic, and named the taboo out loud was now sitting in her front seat and offering her the shape of understanding without asking her to like him for it.
When she answered, the ice was still there, but it had lost some of its sharpness.
“…I know what you mean.” Quiet. Reluctant. Honest enough to cost her. “You walked into a morning where every unknown is a threat to him. I treated you like inventory because that is what the city makes of people who get too close to Seiji. You answered with force because that is what you are. That does not make either of us gentle. It makes us… legible.”
A short breath.
“Understanding is not forgiveness, Curtis Nightstar. And it is not trust. You still have evenings you have not explained. You still have a girl with a scar on her neck in your orbit. You still threw my man into the street.” Her eyes held his. “But I will accept the apology for what it is. And I will not pretend the childhood you just heard is a story I hand to strangers for free.”
She tipped her chin at the cigarette.
“Finish that. Then decide whether this open debt of yours is going to buy me a direction on my brother — or whether we are simply two people who understand each other a little better while he remains missing.”
Smoke curled toward the ceiling.
Curtis had offered understanding without surrender.
Namie had accepted the shape of it without handing him the rest of the map.
The car kept moving through Ikebukuro, and the evening appointments were still waiting for whoever got out first.
Curtis flicked the cigarette out the cracked window and watched the ember vanish into the street.
“Forget the debt earlier.” Long black braids shifted as he settled back against the seat, blood still dry along his hairline, mismatched eyes finding hers without the morning’s edge. “I’ll keep an eye out for Seiji. I’ll help you, Miss Namie.”
The words landed heavier than the gun had.
Namie’s face went still again — not the corporate mask this time, but the genuine lag of someone who had spent the entire ride preparing for leverage, threats, and inventory, and had just been handed voluntary help from the boy who had thrown her security into traffic ten minutes earlier.
“…You are canceling your own price,” she said slowly. “After surviving a gun. After bleeding in my car. After naming things I do not allow strangers to name.”
Curtis shrugged once, small.
“I priced a brother this morning because the morning was hostility and a collar on a sidewalk. You gave me a childhood and a wound with a timeline. That’s enough. I’ll watch for him. If I see him — or the girl with the scar — you’ll hear about it. No open call. No debt. Just eyes in a city that already has too many.”
Namie studied him for a long stretch of traffic.
When she spoke, the ice had thinned into something that almost resembled caution around a new kind of problem.
“…I do not understand you, Curtis Nightstar.” Quiet. Precise. “People do not surrender leverage after they have won it. People do not apologize to the woman who put a gun near their head and then offer to hunt her brother for free.” A short pause. “If this is another performance, it is a costly one. If it is not…”
She looked away first, toward the windshield, as if the admission cost her.
“If it is not, then I will take the help. Quietly. You see him — you tell me. You see the girl — you tell me. You do not play hero in a way that paints a target on either of them, and you do not involve the loud parts of this city unless you have no other choice.” Her eyes cut back to his. “And if you are lying to me with kindness the same way others lie with threats, I will discover it. Do we understand each other?”
Curtis nodded once.
“We do.”
Outside, Ikebukuro kept moving.
Inside, the open debt from the morning sidewalk closed without money changing hands — replaced by something rarer and more dangerous: a foreign student who had decided Namie Yagiri’s brother was worth watching for reasons that had nothing to do with price.
Curtis’s voice stayed even.
“I get a feeling.” Long black braids shifted as mismatched eyes held hers. “There are no heroes in this, however. What you and your company have been doing in the shadows… are you sure you are ready for the blowback? Your company is already facing a decline and debt.”
The car did not swerve. Namie’s hands did not tighten on anything visible. But something behind the ice recalculated again — not the foreign student’s fearlessness this time, but the fact that a college boy with blood on his face had just named the ledger she preferred to keep off the table.
When she answered, the corporate edge returned with the personal one still underneath.
“…You have been listening to the wrong rumors, or the right ones.” Quiet. Precise. “Yagiri Pharmaceuticals does not require a student from Raira to audit its books. Decline is a word the market uses when it wants a softer story than restructuring. Debt is a tool. Shadows are where research happens when the public is not mature enough to fund what it will later beg for.”
A short pause.
“If you are warning me about blowback, understand what you are standing next to. I have already accepted that finding Seiji may cost the company more than a quiet quarter. I have already accepted that certain evenings and certain methods do not survive daylight. What I will not accept is a foreign boy who just canceled his own debt lecturing me on readiness while my brother is still missing.”
Her eyes cut to his.
“You offered to keep an eye out. Do that. Watch for Seiji. Watch for the girl. Leave the shadows and the balance sheets to the people who built them. If blowback comes, it will not be because a student in my front seat predicted it — it will be because this city finally noticed what it had been walking past.”
A thinner beat.
“And if you know more about those shadows than a tourist should, Curtis Nightstar… that is a different conversation. One we can have without cigarettes and crashed peons, if you ever decide the help you offered includes honesty about how much of my company’s dirt is already on your shoes.”
Smoke from the earlier cigarette still clung faintly to the leather.
Curtis had closed the personal debt and opened a corporate one.
Namie had neither confirmed the full weight of the shadows nor denied the decline — only made it clear that the boy who had survived her gun was now close enough to smell the books.
Curtis watched the street slide past the windshield.
“You’re willing to throw away your company for your brother.” Not an accusation. An inventory, spoken quieter than the threats had been. Long black braids shifted as mismatched eyes found hers again. “Have you thought this through, Miss Namie?”
For a stretch of traffic she did not answer.
The question sat in the leather quiet beside the childhood she had already given him — neglect, a study, a piece of Seiji she had been trying to take back ever since. When Namie spoke, the corporate ice and the personal nerve arrived in the same sentence.
“…I thought it through the first time I chose him over a clean quarter.” Precise. Tired in a way the morning sidewalk had not shown. “Yagiri Pharmaceuticals is a structure. Structures can be rebuilt, sold, absorbed, renamed. A brother who walks toward the wrong thing in this city does not get rebuilt if I wait for the books to look respectable.”
A short breath.
“Decline. Debt. Shadows. Blowback. I heard you the first time, Curtis Nightstar. I am not a child playing house with a lab coat. I know what the company has touched. I know what daylight does to it. I know that finding Seiji may cost me the title, the building, and the name on the door.” Her gaze held his. “I have thought it through. The thought ends the same way every time. He is the only thing in that house that was not fake. If the company is the price of reaching him before someone else finishes the work, then the company is already gone. I am simply arriving late to the funeral.”
A thinner pause.
“You offered to keep an eye out. Do that. Do not appoint yourself my conscience about a ledger you have not lived inside. I did not survive this city by being surprised at what I am willing to burn.”
Outside, Ikebukuro kept moving.
Inside, Curtis Nightstar had named the trade she had already made — and Namie Yagiri had not flinched from the cost. Only from the idea that a foreign student in her front seat thought she had not already counted it.
Curtis’s voice stayed low.
“Such a cruel way to live for someone who won’t reciprocate that same love back.” Long black braids shifted as he looked at her without the morning’s edge, without the crash threat, without the inventory smile. “Again… I’m sorry, Miss Namie.”
The words landed differently than the first apology.
Namie had spent years treating kindness as a mask — people who said they loved you only to take, people who offered sympathy as a lever. This boy had put her men on the pavement, survived a gun, thrown a peon into traffic, named the taboo, canceled his own debt, and then sat in her front seat and called her life cruel like it hurt him to watch it.
He isn’t faking.
The thought arrived uninvited and refused to leave.
He means it.
She looked at the blood drying along his hairline, the cigarette gone, the mismatched eyes that had priced her brother before first period and were now offering her grief instead of a bill. For a second the machinery behind her face had nothing useful to do with an input that was not a threat and not a lie.
When she spoke, the ice was thinner than it had been all morning.
“…You do not get to be sorry for a life you did not live.” Quiet. Not sharp. “Seiji does not have to return what I feel. That was never the contract. I keep him breathing. I keep the city from finishing him. What he gives back is his problem.”
A short breath.
“But you—” She stopped, recalculated, and let the rest out anyway. “You are the first person in a long time who said sorry like it was not a negotiation. I do not know what to do with that. Do not make a habit of it. I am not built for comfort from strangers who can walk away from wrecks.”
Her eyes cut to the windshield.
“Keep an eye out for him. That is enough. Do not add pity to the list of things I have to survive today.”
Outside, Ikebukuro did not know a Yagiri sister had just been seen.
Inside, Namie sat with the uncomfortable fact that Curtis Nightstar’s sympathy was real — and that made him more dangerous than the gun had been.
Curtis did not look away.
“I was tortured by my parents until they abandoned me in the Appalachian forest.” The words came out even, like inventory he had already counted once and did not enjoy recounting. Long black braids shifted against the seat. “My mom shot me when I was four. So I do get the apprehension — when someone shows you kindness, you stay on guard and wonder what kind of leverage they’re trying to hold on you.”
A short breath.
“I am not doing that.” Mismatched eyes held hers. “You have never spoken this way to anyone, and it shows.”
The car went quiet in a different register than the gun had produced.
Namie’s face did not crack. It went still the way it had when he named the taboo — then stilled further, because this was not a theory about her brother. This was a four-year-old with a bullet in a forest, offered without a price tag, from the same mouth that had threatened to crash her into a wall twenty minutes earlier.
When she found her voice, it was lower than the corporate ice and less armed than the personal nerve.
“…You were four.” Not a question she wanted the answer to. A fact she did not know how to file. “And you sit in my car and apologize to me for a life I chose.”
Curtis shrugged once, small.
“I sit in your car and recognize the look. Kindness feels like a hook when the last people who were supposed to keep you alive used their hands for something else. I’m telling you I know the look so you don’t have to spend the rest of this ride waiting for the bill. There isn’t one.”
Namie stared at him.
The machinery behind her eyes ran the morning again — sidewalk, debt, gun, backfire, peon in the street, taboo, childhood, company, cruelty — and landed on a foreign student with blood on his face who had just put a bullet from his own mother on the table to prove he was not collecting hers.
“…You are still a very strange boy,” she said at last.
A thinner pause.
“I will take the help. Quietly. If you see Seiji, you tell me. If you see the girl, you tell me.” Her gaze flicked to the blood at his hairline, then away, as if looking too long at either wound would make the kindness real enough to hurt. “Do not tell me more of that forest unless I ask. I am not built for two tragedies in one car.”
Outside, Ikebukuro kept moving.
Inside, two people who treated softness like a weapon had recognized each other’s armor — and neither of them knew yet whether that made the evening safer, or infinitely more complicated.
Namie pulled over.
No announcement. No order. The car simply left the flow of Ikebukuro traffic and settled against the curb like she had decided the blood on his hairline had become a problem she could no longer leave sitting in her front seat.
She found a wet wipe in the console with the same economy she used for everything else — precise, unsentimental — and turned toward him.
Curtis went still.
The wipe met the dried line at his hairline. Long black braids shifted as she worked, careful around the cut, the motion too practiced to be new. For a second her face changed. Not softness. Recognition. A memory overlaying the present: different blood, different boy, a room that had needed cleaning after a night she would not name out loud. She had done this before. She had been the one who arrived when the mess was already made and made it look like a house again.
Curtis’s voice came quiet.
“Thank you, Miss Namie. But you don’t have to do that.”
She did not stop.
“…You are bleeding in my car,” she said, as if that were the entire explanation. The wipe came away rust-brown. “I do not leave unfinished things on leather I have to sit in later.”
Another pass. Gentler than the morning’s gun. Gentler than her mouth.
The memory stayed behind her eyes and did not make it into the sentence. She was not cleaning Seiji. She was not erasing a crime scene. She was wiping a foreign student’s temple because he had apologized twice, canceled his own debt, and told her about a bullet at four years old — and her hands had decided that was close enough to a wound she already knew how to treat.
Curtis did not pull away.
He let her finish the cut, mismatched eyes watching her face more than the wipe, as if he could see the other cleanup sitting just behind this one and had chosen not to name it.
Namie sat back, the used wipe folded small in her fingers.
“…There,” she said. “You look less like a liability.”
A short breath.
“Do not thank me like I did you a kindness. I did what I always do. I made a mess presentable.”
Outside, the city waited.
Inside, Namie Yagiri had pulled over to wipe blood from a boy who was not her brother — and for one unguarded second, she had looked like someone who only knew how to love people by cleaning up after them.
Namie did not sit back all the way.
She looked into his eyes.
The wipe was still folded in her fingers. The cut at his hairline was clean now. Long black braids framed a face that had survived a gun, a hit, and a childhood he had just set on the dashboard like evidence. Up close, the mismatch was impossible to treat as a rumor — two colors that did not belong to the same story, holding still because she had told them to.
“You have nice eyes.”
Curtis blinked.
“What?”
The word came out smaller than anything he had said since the sidewalk. Not a probe. Not a threat. Genuine lag, the same kind she had worn when he first said I’m sorry.
Namie’s expression did not soften into anything safe. It stayed precise, as if she had commented on a specimen and then realized the specimen could hear her.
“…They do not match,” she said, quieter. “Most people would hide that. You do not. It is… noticeable.” A short pause, as if she were already regretting the first sentence. “Do not make it a conversation. I was wiping blood. I looked. That is all.”
She sat back at last, eyes cutting to the windshield like the curb had become safer than his face.
Curtis stayed still for a beat longer, the what? still sitting in the air between them, unused to a Yagiri sister noticing something about him that was not a liability, a debt, or a direction on her brother.
Outside, Ikebukuro waited for the car to rejoin it.
Inside, Namie Yagiri had cleaned his wound and then told the truth about his eyes — and neither of them had a protocol for what to do with that.
Namie stared at the windshield and did not start the car yet.
Why did I say that?
The thought arrived sharp, unwelcome, like a line item that did not belong on the sheet. She had wiped his head because there was blood on leather. That was logistics. That was the same hands that had cleaned other rooms after other boys made messes they could not unmake. That was familiar.
Why did I clean his head?
Not the wipe. The stopping. The pulling over. The care around the cut. The second look into eyes that did not match and the sentence that had left her mouth before the corporate ice could catch it.
You have nice eyes.
She had not said that to Seiji in years. She had not said it to anyone. Kindness was a hook. Compliments were currency. She did not spend either on foreign students who threw her men into traffic and then apologized like it cost them something.
Curtis sat quiet in the passenger seat, still wearing the leftover what? on his face, as if he were waiting to see whether she would take the words back.
Namie did not.
She also did not explain them.
He told you about a bullet at four and you put a wipe on his temple like he was family. That is not a strategy. That is a slip.
Her fingers tightened once on the used wipe, then dropped it into a small bag like evidence she intended to deny later.
“…We are done here,” she said to the glass, not to him. “I will drop you near Raira. You keep an eye out. You tell me if you see him. That is the arrangement.”
The engine found the street again.
Inside her head, the questions kept walking in circles and refusing to clock out.
Why did I say that?
Why did I clean his head?
Outside, Ikebukuro took the car back.
Inside, Namie Yagiri drove like nothing had happened — and hated how clearly she could still see the mismatch of his eyes.
Namie kept her eyes on the road.
“If you need a lift,” she said, voice returned to something almost corporate, “contact me and I will personally give you one. Or have one of my men drive you.”
Curtis glanced at the empty space where the gunman had been.
“I would prefer if you did.” A small, dry beat. “Your men will drive me around with a gun pointed at me.”
The words landed in a place she had not armored for.
Seiji never asked for me personally.
The thought cut clean through the arrangement, through the wipe, through you have nice eyes. Her brother took the help. He took the cleanup. He took the money and the cover and the rooms she made presentable. He did not ask for her in the seat. He did not choose the sister over the men.
Curtis Nightstar, bleeding foreign student with mismatched eyes, had just done it like it was obvious.
Namie’s hands stayed steady on the wheel. She composed herself the way she composed a ledger — line by line, until the crack did not show.
When she spoke, the ice was back in place. Almost.
“…Then you contact me. Not a man. Not a number that routes through a desk.” A short pause. “Do not make a habit of needing to be collected. And do not tell anyone in this city that Namie Yagiri is driving a Raira student around because he objects to firearms in the front seat.”
She did not look at him when she added, quieter:
“I will drop you near campus. After that, you keep your eyes open for Seiji. That is still the arrangement.”
He asked for you. Seiji never did.
She buried the thought before it could become another sentence she had not meant to say.
Outside, Raira’s streets began to show themselves.
Inside, Namie Yagiri had offered a personal lift and been chosen — and she did not know yet whether that made Curtis Nightstar useful, or the most inconvenient person who had sat in her car all year.
Curtis’s gaze dropped to her hands on the wheel.
“Ease up, Namie. I don’t bite.”
Her grip had gone white at the knuckles without her noticing. She loosened it like she was angry at the leather for telling on her.
“Shut up. I know that.”
Curtis chuckled, low and unbothered.
“Alright, Miss Namie.”
The title landed softer than it should have. Namie kept her eyes on the road and hated the small, traitorous fact that she had begun to grow fond of the way he said it — not mocking, not corporate, not the empty respect people used when they wanted something from a Yagiri. Just Miss Namie, like she was a person who could be addressed without being managed.
The college gates came up sooner than the conversation deserved.
She pulled to the curb.
“Remember your promise.”
Curtis popped the door, long black braids shifting as he stepped out into ordinary campus light, the cut at his hairline clean now because she had made it so.
“Ya, ya.” He glanced back, mismatched eyes catching hers one last time. “How about dinner sometime?”
Namie’s face went blank for a full second. Caught. No protocol. No inventory line for a boy who had survived her gun and then asked her to eat.
“…Just find my brother,” she said, recovering into something that sounded like control. “Then we can talk about dinner.”
The window went up. The car pulled away too quickly to be elegant.
She drove confused and conflicted, the wipe still a fact, the compliment still a mistake, Miss Namie still sitting in her ears, and a dinner invitation she had not rejected so much as postponed behind a missing brother.
Curtis stood on the curb and watched the Yagiri car disappear into Ikebukuro traffic.
“She seems nice,” he muttered to himself. “Too bad her love for her brother is fucked up. Justice would’ve loved her.”
Elsewhere — not a street, not a body, just the place the dead still got to be loud —
Justice laughed like he had been waiting for the cue.
“Oh yeah. She’s definitely my type.” A beat, bright and unhelpful. “She can hit me anytime.”
Curtis shook his head, almost smiling, and turned toward campus with a promise in his pocket and a city that had just added Namie Yagiri to the list of people who made staying simple impossible.
